\section{Processing course options for calendar events}
\label{sec:calendar:course-default}

If a course is specified, we get events for that course context.
Otherwise, we get all events for the user.

The course option defaults to the regex [[.*]].
We treat this default as \emph{no explicit course filter} and omit any
context-code parameter, letting Canvas decide which calendars to include.
<<process course option for calendar list>>=
course_list = None
if hasattr(args, 'course') and args.course and args.course != DEFAULT_COURSE_REGEX:
  course_list = courses.process_course_option(canvas, args)
@


\section{Getting and outputting calendar events}

We fetch the calendar events and output them in CSV format.

The Canvas API supports filtering by context codes, but the REST parameter
[[context_codes[]]] is limited to 10 values per request.
Since [[-c/--course]] is a regex, it may match more than 10 courses.
In that case Canvas would silently ignore additional context codes, so we
instead fail fast and ask the user to narrow the regex.

The \texttt{args.type} parameter is optional and may be \texttt{None} by default.
When \texttt{None}, the Canvas API includes all event types (both 
\enquote{event} and \enquote{assignment}).
When specified, it filters to only the requested type.
<<functions>>=
def event_in_any_context(event, context_set):
  """Returns True if the event belongs to any of the contexts in context_set.

  For appointment reservations, Canvas may return context_code as a user
  calendar, while still listing the course context in all_context_codes or
  effective_context_code.
  """
  if not context_set:
    return True

  code = getattr(event, 'context_code', None)
  if code in context_set:
    return True

  code = getattr(event, 'effective_context_code', None)
  if code in context_set:
    return True

  all_codes = getattr(event, 'all_context_codes', None)
  if all_codes:
    for code in all_codes.split(','):
      if code.strip() in context_set:
        return True

  return False

def get_calendar_events(canvas, course_list=None,
                        start_time=None, end_time=None, event_type=None):
  """Fetches calendar events from Canvas with optional filters"""
  params = {}
  if course_list:
    context_codes = [f"course_{course.id}" for course in course_list]
  else:
    context_codes = None
  if start_time:
    params['start_date'] = start_time
  if end_time:
    params['end_date'] = end_time
  if event_type:
    params['type'] = event_type

  if context_codes:
    if len(context_codes) > 10:
      canvaslms.cli.err(
        1,
        "-c/--course matches more than 10 courses, but the Canvas API only "
        "accepts up to 10 context codes per request; narrow the regex "
        "(e.g. '^...$') or match a specific Canvas ID"
      )

    # canvasapi flattens sequences into repeated `context_codes[]` params.
    # Passing `context_codes[]` here would become `context_codes[][]`.
    params['context_codes'] = context_codes

  return canvas.get_calendar_events(**params)
