\subsection{Machine-readable output}

The details we print by default are laid out for a human reader: labelled
lines, a wrapped description, one line per booked participant.  A script
that only wants, say, the URL of every matching event would have to parse
those labels back out again---and any change to the layout would break it.
So we also offer the events as JSON.

<<add calendar view arguments>>=
calendar_view_parser.add_argument("--json",
  action="store_true", default=False,
  help="Output matching events as JSON")
@

What should that JSON contain?  A canvasapi [[CalendarEvent]] is little
more than the JSON object Canvas sent us: [[set_attributes]] turns each
key of the API response into an instance attribute.  Serialising the
object's attribute dictionary therefore hands the consumer back
essentially the event as Canvas described it, ready for [[jq]] or
[[json.loads]].  The alternative we used to take---printing
[[repr(selected)]]---produced a Python list of object reprs, which no
JSON parser accepts, so the [[--json]] flag promised something it never
delivered.

Two properties of that attribute dictionary need care.
The attributes beginning with an underscore are canvasapi's own
bookkeeping rather than data about the event; [[_requester]], which holds
the HTTP session, is not even serialisable.  We drop them.
Timestamps get a derived [[datetime]] twin ([[start_at_date]] alongside
[[start_at]], and so on) that [[json.dumps]] likewise cannot handle on
its own, so we render those with [[default=str]].
Note that this is a weaker conversion than the one [[format_json]]
applies to assignments, where every value is passed through [[str]]:
calendar events carry nested structures---the [[child_events]] of an
appointment group, for instance---and stringifying those would flatten
whole booking lists into single Python reprs.  With [[default=str]] only
the values JSON cannot express are converted, and the nesting survives.

<<functions>>=
def format_events_json(events):
  """Returns a JSON representation of a list of calendar events"""
  return json.dumps([
    {key: value for key, value in event.__dict__.items()
      if not key.startswith("_")}
    for event in events
  ], indent=2, default=str)
@

The human-readable form is the counterpart: one labelled line per piece
of information, expanding an appointment group into the students who
booked its slots.
